Zeolites and zeolite-type materials are of great industrial importance for catalysis, adsorption, separation and ionexchange.[1] Understanding of the mechanism of their synthesis is crucial for further improvement of these materials and rational design of novel ones.[2] In this respect, the development of the techniques for direct monitoring of hydrothermal synthesis in situ is highly desirable.[3] Among the in situ spectroscopic techniques MAS NMR is considered to be one of the most informative,[3, 4] since it is based on the direct observation of atomic nuclei, which compose the frameworks of reagents, products and intermediates and therefore provides a unique advantage of probing the local structure at the molecular level and to access the crystallization process from the very beginning. However, the application of this technique is strongly impeded by a lot of experimental difficulties imposed by severe conditions of hydrothermal synthesis (temperatures up to $473\ \mathsf{K},$ pressure up to 20 bars, aggressive alkaline or acidic media). Therefore the application of this technique for long time was restricted to experiments under static conditions or at relatively low temperature (up to 373 K) and was limited to the studies of few zeotypes.[3,4]
The recent development of specially constructed autoclave-type MAS NMR cells for standard Varian[5] and Bruker[6] MAS NMR equipment opens completely new perspectives for the investigation of hydrothermal synthesis in situ. However, further progress in this field is limited by low natural abundance of $^{29}{\mathsf{S i}}$ and $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ isotopes, preventing time-resolved monitoring of silicon and carbon nuclei, which are of the major importance for zeolite synthesis: the former being the main component of zeolites, the latter being the main component of organic structure directing agent (OSDA) responsible for zeolite structure formation. Therefore isotopic labeling of silicon and carbon nuclei is crucial for further progress in this field.
Herein we report on the first attempt for the time-resolved monitoring of zeolite BEA synthesis by means of in situ $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ and $^{29}\mathrm{Si}$ MAS NMR along with $^{23}\mathsf{N a}$ and $^{27}\mathsf{A l}$ MAS NMR. Isotopically labelled silica gel and TEAOH $C^{13}C H_{3}-C H_{2})_{4}N O H)$ are used to achieve the goal. Zeolite BEA is selected due to several reasons. First of all, the synthesis of zeolite BEA can be considered as a remarkable test bed for in situ mechanistic studies. Various mechanisms have been proposed to account for its nucleation and growth,[2,7,8,9] however the reasons for different mechanisms operation remain unclear. Furthermore, the synthesis conditions for zeolite BEA are well representative for a wide variety of zeolites, as well as other ordered porous solids, making the impact of the results broader and applicable to a variety of materials. Finally, zeolite BEA is an excellent catalyst and sorbent for a broad range of industrial processes[10a] such as cumene synthesis, C2/C3 transalkylation, acylation and depollution/decontamination processes (SCR of NOx,  VOC adsorption). Besides that, it shows high potential for the transformation and valorization of bio-sourced substrates.[10b] Two procedures are selected for BEA synthesis. The first procedure is taken from the work of Perez-Pariente group.[8] The second recipe is adopted from the paper coming from Montpellier group[9]. The reason for selecting these two procedures is due to different mechanisms of synthesis claimed by the authors, namely, solution mediated in the former case[7] and solid-solid transformation in the latter.[8] Our goal was to verify whether these mechanisms can be distinguished by in situ MAS NMR and to reveal which novel chemistry can be disclosed by this method to get deeper insight into the reasons for different mechanisms operation.
The schematic representation of the procedures is given in Fig. 1, the details being provided in Supporting information (SI). The main difference of two procedures is in the order of reactant mixing: whereas in the first procedure Si source is added at the beginning, in the second case it is added at the end. The detailed description of the in situ MAS NMR experiments, their comparison with ex situ studies, the procedures used for the synthesis of isotopically labelled silica gel and TEAOH ( $1^{13}{\mathsf{C H}}_{3}.$ ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2})_{4}\mathsf{N O H})$ and the schematic representation of the autoclavetype MAS NMR cell are provided in SI (Figs. S1-S4).
The first step of the study was focused on the initial gel formation. The selected NMR spectra obtained during two procedures are compared in Fig. 1. The results show that changing of the order of reactants mixing leads to completely different gels. In the first case (Fig.1, Procedure I), the addition of Si source at the initial steps of reactants mixing results in formation of siliceous gel, containing alkali rich $\ensuremath{\mathsf{M}}^{+}/\ensuremath{\mathsf{S i}}_{\mathsf{x}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{O}}_{\mathsf{y}}$ composites. $\mathsf{T E A}^{+}$ cations remain in solution, as evidenced by narrow $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ MAS NMR signal at ca. 7.6 ppm and broad $^{23}{\mathsf{N a}}$ resonance line at ca. 1ppm. Further addition of Al-source leads to rapid precipitation of Al-rich $\mathsf{M}^{+}/\mathsf{A l S i}_{\mathsf{x}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{y}}$ gel.
The charge on Al species in this gel is most probably compensated by ${N a^{+}}$ and $\mathsf{K}^{+}$ , whereas TEA+ cations are preserved in solution, as confirmed by $^{23}{\mathsf{N a}}$ , $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ and $^{27}\mathsf{A l}$ MAS NMR (Fig.1). In contrast, addition of Si source at the later steps of mixing (Fig.1, Procedure II) results in the formation of TEA+- containing aluminosilicate gel, as evidenced by the appearance of the broad signal at ca. 8.8 ppm in the $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ CP/MAS NMR spectrum and intense signals at ca. -98 $(\mathsf{Q}_{3})$ and - $-108\ p p\mathsf{m}\left(\mathsf{Q}_{4}\right)$ in $^{29}\mathrm{Si}$ CP/MAS NMR due to the cross-polarization of Si nuclei with the protons of TEA in $\mathsf{T E A^{+}/A l S i_{x}O_{y}}$ composites (Fig. 1). This gel has lower content of Al with respect to the first one as can be seen from $^{27}\mathsf{A l}$ MAS NMR spectra (Fig. 1). The position of the NMR lines corresponding to Al species in two gels is different due to different local surroundings of Al: whereas in the first gel it is interacting with $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ and ${\sf K}^{+}$ cations, in the second one it is attached to TEA .
The time-resolved MAS NMR spectra of hydrothermal crystallization of two gels are shown in Fig. 2. The results point to different mechanisms operating. In the first case, zeolite BEA is crystallized from solution species as clearly demonstrated by $^{29}\mathrm{Si}$ MAS NMR (Fig. 2a). $^{29}{\mathsf{S i}}$ MAS NMR signals corresponding to $Q_{1}$ $(77-83\ \mathsf{p p m})$ ), $Q_{2}$ (86-91 ppm), $Q_{3}$ (93-102 ppm), ${\sf Q}_{4}$ (104- $120~\mathsf{p p m},$ ) mobile species are broadened but still well resolved, which points to fast exchange between the liquid and solid phases. During crystallization these signals disappear at the expense of the new signal at ca. -109.4 ppm, which is amenable to cross-polarization (Fig. S5) and corresponds to zeolite BEA.
$^{27}\mathsf{A l}$ MAS NMR spectra confirm the solution mediated mechanism pointing to the gradual dissolution of initial Al-rich $\mathsf{M}^{+}/\mathsf{A l S i}_{\mathsf{x}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{y}}$ phase characterized by the signal at ca. 60 ppm, (Fig. 2c), followed by the appearance of growing signal at ca. 54.4 ppm corresponding to zeolite BEA. The formation of zeolite BEA is also confirmed by the signal at ca. 7.1 ppm in $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ CP MAS NMR (Fig. 2e), corresponding to methyl groups of TEA in zeolite BEA.[11] The latter signal is preceded by the observation of the narrow NMR line at ca. 7.6 ppm corresponding to mobile $\mathsf{T E A}^{+}$ cations interacting with aluminosilicate species in solution and a broad NMR line at ca. 8.1 ppm, which in accordance with Ikuno et al.[9] can be attributed to $\mathsf{T E A}^{+}$ clusters in TEA+/aluminosilicate composite precursors of zeolite BEA.
Completely different picture is observed in the case of the second gel crystallization. 29Si MAS NMR spectra (Fig. 2b) suggest that heating of the gel leads to the formation of solid amorphous hydrogel, represented by very broad signal in the range of -70 - -110 ppm, which is converted into zeolite BEA via solid-solid hydrogel rearrangement. $^{27}\mathsf{A l}$ MAS NMR results (Fig. 2d) are in line with that, showing gradual transformation of the NMR line at ca. 52.8 ppm into the signal at ca. 54.4 ppm corresponding to zeolite BEA.
$^{13}\mathsf{C}$ CP MAS NMR spectra (Fig. 2f) suggest that part of $\mathsf{T E A}^{+}$ cations is incorporated in solid amorphous hydrogel from the very beginning of synthesis as confirmed by the appearance of the signal at ca. 8.6 ppm, corresponding to rigid species involved in solid state interactions. This signal is disappearing at the expense of the signal at ca. 8.1 ppm assigned to TEA+/aluminosilicate composite precursors of zeolite BEA. These observations are line with previous findings by Nicolle et al.[8], suggesting the formation of tetraethylammonium permutite as an intermediate in zeolite BEA synthesis.
The kinetic curves of zeolite BEA crystallization built on the basis of $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ , $^{27}\mathsf{A l}$ and $^{29}{\mathsf{S i}}$ MAS NMR data are in good agreement between each other (Fig. S7).The comparison of the kinetic data obtained for two reaction procedures is shown in Fig. 3. The results point that solid-solid rearrangement leads to shorter induction period, which could be due to pre-formation of TEA+/aluminosilicate composites at the very early steps of synthesis.
The model presented in Fig. 4 further rationalizes our observations and the conclusions of previous reports.[7,8]
