The first procedure leads to rapid precipitation of Al-rich $\mathsf{M}^{+}/$ /aluminosilicate particles. $\mathsf{T E A}^{+}$ cations remain in the solution as confirmed by $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ MAS NMR. Heating of the gel results in the dissolution of M+/aluminosilicate composites, the interaction of dissolved species with $\mathsf{T E A}^{+}$ and the formation of mobile TEA+/AlSi species in solution (1). The latter further aggregate into larger $\mathsf{T E A}^{+}$ clusters, which leads to formation of larger $\mathsf{T E A^{+}/M^{+}},$ /aluminosilicate composites and zeolite BEA nuclei (2). Further heating results in solution mediated crystal growth of zeolite BEA (3). The second procedure leads to the formation of an amorphous hydrogel with occluded $\mathsf{T E A}^{+}$ cations. These cations are responsible for further solid-solid rearrangement into zeolite BEA precursors via migration and clustering (1) leading to further nucleation (2) and crystal growth (3).
The mechanisms depicted in Fig. 4 are in line with those previously proposed by Perez-Pariente et al.[7], Nicolle et al.[8] and Ikuno et al.[9] on the basis of indirect methods including synthesis interruption, intermediate products isolation and analysis. In this contribution, we provide for the first direct demonstration of these mechanisms basing on the results of the time-resolved $^{29}\mathrm{Si}$ MAS NMR and $^{29}\mathrm{Si}$ -CP MAS NMR in situ. Furthermore, this study shows that in situ MAS NMR techniques allows for a deeper understanding of the initial stages of hydrothermal synthesis and the reasons of different mechanisms operation. This information is obtained by monitoring the structure directing behavior of $\mathsf{T E A}^{+}$ cations at the very early steps of synthesis. Different TEA species have been identified and the mechanism of their interaction with other species has been established. These results are of prime importance for the further development of novel BEA-based materials (Sn-BEA, ZrBEA, Ti-BEA), attracting broad attention during the last years[10b], as well as, for the other TEA-templated zeolites and zeotypes of different structural types (LTA, MFI, AEI, AFI, CHA, MOR and etc.) [12].
To conclude, the potential of the in situ multinuclear MAS NMR for the elucidation of the mechanism of hydrothermal synthesis has been fully demonstrated. It is shown that $^{29}\mathrm{Si}$ and $^{29}\mathrm{Si}$ -CP MAS NMR permits detecting various mobile and rigid silicate and alumosilicate species in solution and solid state and monitoring their transformation into zeolite. $^{27}\mathsf{A l}$ MAS NMR is very sensitive to the interaction with inorganic and organic cations and provides information on the involvement of different cations in aluminosilicate species at different steps of synthesis. $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ and $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ -CP MAS NMR gives extremely important information on the structure directing behavior of OSDA. All together the spectral data give insight into the mechanism of the main steps of synthesis, including, gel formation, gel aging, crystal nucleation and growth; provide information on the kinetics of each reaction step; enable detection intermediate species stable only under hydrothermal conditions.
The main advantage of in situ MAS NMR is that it provides information at the very early steps of synthesis and allows for deeper understanding of the reasons of different mechanisms operation. This technique can be used without interrupting the synthesis and perturbing the crystallization process, it reduces the time and minimizes the amount of sample required for mechanistic studies by several orders of magnitude and allows for isotopic labelling of specific nuclei. All this indicates that this technique can serve as a superior tool for monitoring of hydrothermal synthesis of broad range of solids including zeolites, zeotypes, mesoporous materials, metal organic frameworks and etc. and for the design of novel remarkable materials for different applications.
